from (values
('127.16.d.4.D',2,'Choosing PPE should be based on:','["color preferences", "price alone", "what a coworker wears", "the hazards present in the task"]'::jsonb,3,'The hazard determines the PPE.','original'),
('127.16.d.4.E',2,'Protective gear for the head, hands and feet is meant to:','["guard parts of the body from workplace injury", "replace safe habits", "hide the worker", "cause discomfort"]'::jsonb,0,'PPE protects specific body areas.','original'),
('127.16.d.4.E',2,'Steel-toed boots are worn to protect:','["the ears from noise", "the feet from falling or rolling objects", "the lungs from fumes", "the eyes from dust"]'::jsonb,1,'Boots prevent crush injuries.','original'),
('127.16.d.4.F',3,'A common cause of an incident is:','["proper training", "clear labels", "an unsafe act or unsafe condition", "good planning"]'::jsonb,2,'Incidents come from unsafe acts and conditions.','original'),
('127.16.d.4.F',3,'Identifying the cause of an incident helps to:','["hide the facts", "ignore the lesson", "assign blame only", "prevent it from happening again"]'::jsonb,3,'Causes guide corrective actions.','original'),
('127.16.d.4.G',2,'A comprehensive safety program is necessary because it:','["covers hazards across all work activities", "replaces laws", "applies to one worker", "only covers paperwork"]'::jsonb,0,'Broad programs protect everyone.','original'),
('127.16.d.4.G',2,'A safety program works best when it is:','["ignored", "consistently followed and reviewed", "unchanged for decades", "secret"]'::jsonb,1,'Programs need regular review.','original'),
('127.16.d.4.H',2,'Good housekeeping means:','["storing waste in aisles", "stacking boxes in exits", "keeping the workplace orderly and clean", "leaving tools on the floor"]'::jsonb,2,'Order prevents accidents.','original'),
('127.16.d.4.H',2,'Which housekeeping practice reduces fire risk?','["blocking exits", "storing paper near heaters", "letting rags pile up", "removing trash and combustible waste promptly"]'::jsonb,3,'Clutter feeds fires.','original'),
('127.16.d.4.I',3,'Debris on the floor can lead to:','["trips and falls", "safer walking", "better lighting", "faster cleaning"]'::jsonb,0,'Debris creates trip hazards.','original'),
('127.16.d.4.I',3,'A disorganized workplace increases accident risk because:','["workers feel calmer", "hazards are harder to see and avoid", "exits are clearer", "tools are easier to find"]'::jsonb,1,'Disorder hides hazards.','original'),
('127.16.d.5.A',3,'Which is a workplace security event?','["a software update", "a lunch delivery", "unauthorized entry into a building", "a scheduled meeting"]'::jsonb,2,'Security events threaten people or property.','original'),
('127.16.d.5.A',3,'Theft of company property is classified as:','["a routine delivery", "a safety drill", "a training class", "a workplace security event"]'::jsonb,3,'It threatens organizational security.','original'),
('127.16.d.5.B',3,'A strategy to improve workplace security is:','["controlling access with badges or keys", "propping doors open", "sharing passwords", "leaving areas unlocked"]'::jsonb,0,'Access control limits entry.','original'),
('127.16.d.5.B',3,'Security awareness training helps employees to:','["ignore visitors", "recognize and report suspicious activity", "share codes", "avoid policy"]'::jsonb,1,'Aware employees reduce risks.','original'),
('127.16.d.5.C',3,'A strategy to prevent workplace violence is:','["hiding conflict", "ignoring warning signs", "a clear policy and a way to report threats", "punishing reports"]'::jsonb,2,'Policies and reporting reduce violence.','original'),
('127.16.d.5.C',3,'Early warning signs of workplace violence should be:','["posted online", "kept secret", "laughed off", "reported to management"]'::jsonb,3,'Early reports allow intervention.','original'),
('127.16.d.6.A',2,'Ergonomics is the science of:','["fitting the job to the worker", "timing lunch", "choosing office colors", "fitting the worker to any job"]'::jsonb,0,'It designs work to suit people.','original'),
('127.16.d.6.A',2,'The goal of ergonomics is to:','["speed up injuries", "reduce strain and injury", "limit comfort", "increase fatigue"]'::jsonb,1,'Good design protects the body.','original'),
('127.16.d.6.B',3,'Ergonomics is used in construction to:','["increase lifting weight", "remove breaks", "design tools and tasks that reduce strain", "raise noise"]'::jsonb,2,'It applies in many industries.','original'),
('127.16.d.6.B',3,'A manufacturing line that adjusts work height for different workers is applying:','["inventory counting", "taxation", "advertising", "ergonomics"]'::jsonb,3,'Adjusting heights reduces strain.','original'),
('127.16.d.6.C',3,'Which body position can cause an ergonomic problem?','["repeated bending or twisting", "adjustable seating", "neutral posture", "short breaks"]'::jsonb,0,'Awkward posture strains the body.','original'),
('127.16.d.6.C',3,'Repeating the same motion all day can lead to:','["better strength only", "a repetitive motion injury", "less fatigue", "faster healing"]'::jsonb,1,'Repetition causes strain over time.','original'),
('127.16.d.6.D',3,'Poor ergonomics most often affects which body system?','["the hair and nails", "the nervous system only", "the musculoskeletal system", "the digestive system only"]'::jsonb,2,'Muscles, bones and joints are strained.','original'),
('127.16.d.6.D',3,'Carpal tunnel syndrome involves strain to the:','["knee only", "lungs", "foot only", "wrist and hand"]'::jsonb,3,'It is caused by repeated hand motions.','original'),
('127.16.d.6.E',3,'Physical fatigue at work can be produced by:','["heavy lifting and long hours without rest", "short shifts", "light tasks only", "frequent breaks"]'::jsonb,0,'Overexertion causes fatigue.','original'),
('127.16.d.6.E',3,'A way to reduce fatigue in a physical task is to:','["skip meals", "schedule rest breaks and rotate tasks", "double the load", "remove breaks"]'::jsonb,1,'Rest and rotation limit fatigue.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.A',3,'Industrial hygiene plays a role in safety by:','["delaying inspections", "designing logos", "identifying health hazards in the workplace", "increasing hazards"]'::jsonb,2,'It evaluates and controls hazards.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.A',3,'Industrial hygienists measure worker exposure to:','["payroll", "traffic tickets", "lunch menus", "noise, chemicals and other hazards"]'::jsonb,3,'Measurements guide controls.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.B',3,'One method to identify hazards is to:','["review the chemical inventory and conduct walk-throughs", "ignore records", "wait for accidents", "guess"]'::jsonb,0,'Reviews reveal hazards.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.B',3,'Interviewing workers about their tasks helps find hazards because:','["it hides problems", "workers know the risks of their jobs", "workers know nothing", "it replaces inspections"]'::jsonb,1,'Worker input is valuable.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.C',3,'Industrial hygiene hazards are grouped into categories such as:','["north, south and east", "red, green and blue", "chemical, physical, biological and ergonomic", "early, middle and late"]'::jsonb,2,'These categories organize exposures.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.C',3,'Radiation and extreme heat are examples of:','["biological hazards", "paperwork", "legal hazards", "physical hazards"]'::jsonb,3,'Physical hazards come from energy sources.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.D',3,'Mold and bacteria are examples of:','["biological hazards", "physical hazards", "ergonomic hazards", "chemical hazards"]'::jsonb,0,'They are living agents.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.D',3,'Lifting heavy loads repeatedly is an example of an:','["biological hazard", "ergonomic hazard", "legal hazard", "chemical hazard"]'::jsonb,1,'It strains the body.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.E',3,'Breathing a toxic chemical for years may lead to:','["instant benefits", "no effects ever", "long-term health effects such as lung disease", "stronger lungs"]'::jsonb,2,'Chronic exposure has lasting effects.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.E',3,'Hazardous substances can be classified as:','["silent or loud", "old or new", "big or small", "toxic, corrosive, flammable or reactive"]'::jsonb,3,'These categories describe hazards.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.F',4,'An acute exposure is one that:','["happens briefly at a high level", "happens only at home", "lasts for decades", "is always harmless"]'::jsonb,0,'Acute exposures are short and intense.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.F',4,'A chronic exposure is one that:','["occurs once", "is repeated over a long period", "has no effect", "only happens at night"]'::jsonb,1,'Chronic exposures accumulate over time.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.G',3,'A supervisor''s essential safety responsibility is to:','["avoid inspections", "blame workers", "enforce safety rules and correct hazards", "ignore reports"]'::jsonb,2,'Supervisors lead prevention.','original'),
('127.16.d.7.G',3,'Safety personnel help prevent injuries by:','["skipping audits", "hiding data", "avoiding site visits", "training workers and monitoring hazards"]'::jsonb,3,'They support safe operations.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.A',3,'The Hazard Communication Standard requires employers to provide:','["labels, safety data sheets and training", "extra vacation", "free lunches", "higher wages"]'::jsonb,0,'Workers must be informed of chemical hazards.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.A',3,'The Hazard Communication Standard aims to ensure that workers:','["never use chemicals", "know the hazards of chemicals they use", "avoid training", "work alone"]'::jsonb,1,'Right-to-know protects workers.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.B',3,'To learn the first aid measures for a chemical, a worker reads:','["a menu", "a time card", "the safety data sheet", "an invoice"]'::jsonb,2,'SDS include first aid information.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.B',3,'A container label should show:','["a sports score", "the owner''s address only", "a coupon", "the product name and hazard warnings"]'::jsonb,3,'Labels warn users of hazards.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.C',3,'Proper chemical storage prevents:','["dangerous reactions between incompatible chemicals", "all training", "inventory counts", "all paperwork"]'::jsonb,0,'Incompatible chemicals must be separated.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.C',3,'Acids and bases should be stored:','["together on one shelf", "separately from each other", "in open trays", "in food refrigerators"]'::jsonb,1,'Mixing can cause violent reactions.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.D',3,'A flash point describes:','["a color of paint", "a boiling water level", "the temperature at which a liquid gives off ignitable vapor", "a camera feature"]'::jsonb,2,'It shows flammability.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.D',3,'A corrosive material:','["feeds plants", "cleans nothing", "cools air", "destroys living tissue or metal on contact"]'::jsonb,3,'Corrosives damage materials and skin.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.E',3,'Hazardous materials can enter the body through:','["inhalation, skin contact and ingestion", "only sound", "only sight", "only touch with gloves"]'::jsonb,0,'These are the main routes of entry.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.E',3,'Absorption is when a chemical enters the body through the:','["shoes only", "skin", "clothes tags", "hair color"]'::jsonb,1,'Some chemicals pass through skin.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.F',3,'To protect against inhaling harmful dust, a worker may use:','["ear plugs", "a belt", "a respirator", "sunglasses"]'::jsonb,2,'Respirators filter air.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.F',3,'Local exhaust ventilation reduces airborne contaminants by:','["hiding the smell", "spreading them", "making them heavier", "capturing them near the source"]'::jsonb,3,'It removes contaminants before inhalation.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.G',3,'Compressed gas cylinders should be:','["secured upright and capped when not in use", "rolled on their sides", "stored near heat", "left uncapped"]'::jsonb,0,'Falling cylinders are dangerous.','original'),
('127.16.d.8.G',3,'A compressed gas cylinder with a damaged valve could:','["float away", "become a projectile", "stop rolling", "become safer"]'::jsonb,1,'Release of gas can propel the cylinder.','original')
